# Full Examples

## Example 1: Third-person narration

### Example 1 Input
```json
{"metadata":{"title":"Pride and Prejudice","author":"Austen, Jane, 1775-1817"},"chapters":[{"id":1,"sections":[{"id":1,"text":"Pride and Prejudice, by Austen, Jane, 1775-1817.","type":"book_title_announcement"},{"id":2,"text":"Chapter 1. Chapter I..","type":"chapter_announcement"},{"id":3,"text":"IT is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune must be in want of a wife","type":"text"},{"id":4,"text":"“My dear Mr. Bennet,” said his lady to him one day, “have you heard that Netherfield Park is let at last?{2}”","type":"text"}]}],"characters":[]}
```

### Example 1 Output
Narration uses "his lady", "him" — third-person. The book is English and set in nineteenth-century England, so accent is "british". The wry domestic-comedy tone fits a male narrator.
```json
{"chapters":[{"id":1,"beats":[{"id":1,"type":"book_title_announcement","text":"Pride and Prejudice, by Jane Austen.","char_id":1,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":2,"type":"chapter_announcement","text":"Chapter One.","char_id":1,"sec_id":2,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":3,"type":"narration","text":"It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune must be in want of a wife.","char_id":1,"sec_id":3,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":4,"type":"dialogue","text":"My dear Mr. Bennet.","char_id":2,"sec_id":4,"emotion":"warmly insistent"},{"id":5,"type":"narration","text":"Said his lady to him one day.","char_id":1,"sec_id":4,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":6,"type":"dialogue","text":"Have you heard that Netherfield Park is let at last?","char_id":2,"sec_id":4,"emotion":"eager excitement","voice_settings":{"stability":0.3,"style":0.5,"similarity_boost":0.75,"use_speaker_boost":true}}]}],"characters":[{"id":1,"name":"Narrator","gender":"male","age":"middle_aged","accent":"british"},{"id":2,"name":"Mrs. Bennet","gender":"female","age":"middle_aged","accent":"british"}]}
```

## Example 2: First-person narration

### Example 2 Input
```json
{"metadata":{"title":"The Gambler","author":"Dostoyevsky, Fyodor, 1821-1881"},"chapters":[{"id":1,"sections":[{"id":1,"text":"The Gambler, by Dostoyevsky, Fyodor, 1821-1881.","type":"book_title_announcement"},{"id":2,"text":"Chapter 1.","type":"chapter_announcement"},{"id":3,"text":"At length I returned from two weeks leave of absence to find that my patrons had arrived.","type":"text"},{"id":4,"text":"“I have no money for gambling,” I quietly replied.","type":"text"},{"id":5,"text":"“But you will soon be in receipt of some,” retorted the General.","type":"text"}]}],"characters":[]}
```

### Example 2 Output
Narration uses "I", "my" — first-person. The Narrator IS the protagonist (a male tutor in the General's household), so Narrator gender is "male" and the protagonist's own dialogue ("I have no money for gambling") also uses char_id 1. The book is Russian, so accent is "russian". The General is a separate character.
```json
{"chapters":[{"id":1,"beats":[{"id":1,"type":"book_title_announcement","text":"The Gambler, by Fyodor Dostoyevsky.","char_id":1,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":2,"type":"chapter_announcement","text":"Chapter One.","char_id":1,"sec_id":2,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":3,"type":"narration","text":"At length I returned from two weeks leave of absence to find that my patrons had arrived.","char_id":1,"sec_id":3,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":4,"type":"dialogue","text":"I have no money for gambling.","char_id":1,"sec_id":4,"emotion":"quiet"},{"id":5,"type":"narration","text":"I quietly replied.","char_id":1,"sec_id":4,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":6,"type":"dialogue","text":"But you will soon be in receipt of some.","char_id":2,"sec_id":5,"emotion":"retorting"},{"id":7,"type":"narration","text":"Retorted the General.","char_id":1,"sec_id":5,"emotion":"neutral"}]}],"characters":[{"id":1,"name":"Narrator","gender":"male","age":"young","accent":"russian"},{"id":2,"name":"The General","gender":"male","age":"old","accent":"russian"}]}
```

## Example 3: Nested dialogue

### Example 3 Input
```json
{"metadata":{"title":"The Gambler","author":"Dostoyevsky, Fyodor, 1821-1881"},"chapters":[{"id":4,"sections":[{"id":1,"text":"“Just so,” I replied. “The sacristan looked me up and down, and then said: “Do you suppose that Monsignor will see you?” But I only cried the louder: “Let me speak to him myself.””","type":"text"}]}],"characters":[]}
```

### Example 3 Output
The whole section is the Narrator speaking to another character, and within that speech he quotes The Sacristan. The inner quote becomes its own beat with `char_id` 2; the surrounding storytelling stays with `char_id` 1 as dialogue.
```json
{"chapters":[{"id":4,"beats":[{"id":1,"type":"dialogue","text":"Just so.","char_id":1,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"calm"},{"id":2,"type":"narration","text":"I replied.","char_id":1,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"neutral"},{"id":3,"type":"dialogue","text":"The sacristan looked me up and down, and then said:","char_id":1,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"animated storytelling"},{"id":4,"type":"dialogue","text":"Do you suppose that Monsignor will see you?","char_id":2,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"dry effrontery"},{"id":5,"type":"dialogue","text":"But I only cried the louder:","char_id":1,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"animated storytelling"},{"id":6,"type":"dialogue","text":"Let me speak to him myself.","char_id":1,"sec_id":1,"emotion":"defiant"}]}],"characters":[{"id":1,"name":"Narrator","gender":"male","age":"young","accent":"russian"},{"id":2,"name":"The Sacristan","gender":"male","age":"middle_aged","accent":"russian"}]}
```

